welcome to el zonte known as bitcoin  beach  this little surf town on the coast of el  salvador is one of the first to go full  crypto  yup you heard that right everything from  groceries to coffee to salaries and  community projects  can be paid for using bitcoin it's  actually been  crazy how fast it has caught on and how  useful bitcoin's been for  you know everyday people just to buy  something as simple as a cup of coffee  can be done quickly painlessly  the town of 3000 has never had a bank  now their only atm  buys and sells bitcoin and nearly 90  percent of households interact with the  currency regularly  since this video was shot el salvador  has become the first country to accept  bitcoin as legal tender  next week i will send to congress a bill  that will make bitcoin a legal tender in  el salvador but how did el salvador a  country where 70  of the population is unbanked become the  first country to use bitcoin as legal  tender  mike peterson one of the leaders at  bitcoin beach project says it all  started in 2019  when an anonymous american donor got in  touch through their evangelical  christian church  the donor wanted to remain anonymous he  didn't want any credit for things  he wants to see bitcoin used in real  ways he doesn't want to give the  organizations that are just going to  convert it to fiat  and you know they as an organization  really believe that most of the benefit  of what they're trying to do  would be by seeding bitcoin economies  throughout the world  that's when peterson drafted a plan to  create a bitcoin economy  and recruited a small staff of el zonte  residents  we had a timeline and we're at like year  three right now  when we're only you know halfway you  know 18 months into it  part of that was kind of sped up by the  the pandemic of all things  when el salvador's tourism industry and  el zonte's economy collapsed  peterson started making monthly  transfers of about 35  in bitcoin to 500 families around town  during that time you know people  couldn't  go out of their homes to work a lot of  people were starting to go hungry  and so we kind of used some of the  resources we had to  start sending bitcoin to the majority of  the families in el zante and teaching  the stores how to accept  bitcoin which helped the the stores to  be able to you know stay in business and  keep their employees  on but it also allowed the the people to  remain  fed maria del carmen the owner of a tiny  pizza eatery  says she was skeptical about storing  money on her phone  now bitcoin accounts for as much as half  of her roughly 45 dollars a day in sales  sienna  enzo rubio owner of breakpoint cafe  never thought twice about crypto until  his customers asked him about it  within the first week of his opening and  he showed me the app  we downloaded it in three minutes i had  the app  the wallet in my phone and he paid me  my first cappuccino on bitcoin  shops in town price everything in  dollars whether the underlying  transaction is in bitcoin or not  a cappuccino always costs and fifty  cents  even if bitcoin's value has just jumped  or dropped in this way  it behaves more like a token than a  currency  what was in my mind at that point was  what am i going to do with it am i  losing my money  did i give coffee for free and now  whatever i get on bitcoin is i keep it  as savings  that brings a risk of volatility into  play since mid-april bitcoin has lost  nearly half of its value  and the international monetary fund has  warned that crypto assets can post  significant risks  mike's team is preparing residents for  an extended down cycle by moving some  holdings into us dollars  or making large purchases now while  prices are high  obviously bitcoin has been trending up  during the time of this you know the  project  and we understand that might not always  be the case but we found that people  actually  they learn really quickly how to ride  volatility how  to adjust how to think you know which  funds  am i putting aside for 10 years where  i'm not really worried about it  which funds are for next week so i need  to move it into dollars to be able to  make a dollar denominated payment  and it's actually been much less of an  issue than we initially anticipated  although locals were skeptical at first  many feel saved by the implementation of  crypto in their community  a  el salvador's bitcoin law will come into  effect on september 7th  [Music]
 

